"""
=======================================================================
  International Olympiad on Astronomy and Astrophysics 2019 (Keszthely)
  Night Observation Round
  Virtual observing field
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
  HaslaLab  /  HaslaEdu        https://haslalab.org
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
  Single file, Tkinter, Python standard library only.

Design rule (non-negotiable):
    The sky is computed, not painted: the students' field at the southern
    edge of Keszthely (46.755 N, 17.245 E, CEST = UTC+2) on the night of
    4 or 5 August 2019 from 23:42 CEST, running in real time.  Stars come
    from Hipparcos (to 6 mag) and Tycho-2 (to 9.3 along the route of the
    round and to 12.5 around the targets); fainter field stars elsewhere
    are drawn from star counts.  The Sun, the Moon and the planets follow
    their orbits; Saturn shows its rings at the true tilt and its moons
    (Titan's phase fitted to the official positions for both nights).
    Extinction and a sky glow towards the town; the weather is each
    night's own, from the ERA5 reanalysis hour by hour: on 4 August a
    clear, very humid night whose low haze over the lake hides Saturn -
    then tasks 1 and 2 become the official alternatives - and on 5 August
    a veil of cirrus that breaks up after midnight.  No compass marks,
    no pointer, no lines on the sky.  F (or the button) puts the sky on
    the whole screen.  The eye sees only the stars above its limit, the
    faintest of them only now and then, and does not zoom.  Around the
    field stands the land as it is there: the town and its church spire
    to the north, the Keszthely Hills, Lake Balaton with Badacsony on the
    far shore, the marsh to the south and the other teams' telescopes.

    The telescope is the one of the round: a 150/750 Newtonian on a
    polar-aligned equatorial mount without a drive, a 6x30 finder that is
    not aligned, eyepieces 25 mm and 10 mm, a 2x Barlow, a focuser that
    starts out of focus.  The tasks run on their own clocks and the
    assistant checks the telescope at the end of each, as in the round;
    the answer sheet is marked with the official scheme.

    Run:   python3 ioaa2019_O1_sky_sim_KO.py   [--seed N]
"""
